      }
    }
  ],
  "removableIds": [],
  "narration": [
    {
      "id": "brief",
      "text": "Under Lock, back at Pé na Areia. Visitors upload receipts to an S3 bucket and look up orders in an RDS database, behind the network ACL and WAF from last time. An auditor has two worries: who can read the receipts, and who holds the keys that protect the data. Read the bucket's card, then fly.",
      "highlight": "button:fly",
      "advanceWhen": {
        "kind": "run-finished"
      }
    },
    {
      "id": "what-happened",
      "text": "The hooded Cloudling read every receipt straight from the bucket: a breach, and its string is still attached. edge-acl block-lists its address, but S3 is outside the VPC, so no network ACL or WAF stands in its way. The bucket is public because Block Public Access is off and its policy lets everyone read.",
      "highlight": "resource:receipts",
      "advanceWhen": {
        "kind": "acknowledged"
      }
    },
    {
      "id": "close",
      "text": "Close the bucket. On its card, switch Block Public Access on, and untick the policy that lets everyone read: Block Public Access overrides it, but a permission nobody needs is waiting for the day someone switches it off. Then fly.",
      "highlight": "resource:receipts",
      "pulse": ["bucket-bpa:receipts"],
      "advanceWhen": {
        "kind": "run-finished",
        "predicate": {
          "kind": "flows-stopped",
          "flows": {
            "flowIds": ["red-reader"]
          }
        }
      }
    },
    {
      "id": "key",
      "text": "The red kite is turned away. The receipts were never unencrypted: since January 2023 S3 encrypts every new object (SSE-S3) with keys S3 manages. The auditor wants a key the shop controls, with every use logged. Drag a KMS key from the tray: a customer managed key.",
      "highlight": "palette:shop-key",
      "advanceWhen": {
        "kind": "scene",
        "predicate": {
          "kind": "resource-exists",
          "resourceId": "shop-key"
        }
      }
    },
    {
      "id": "bucket-kms",
      "text": "On the bucket's card, set Default encryption to SSE-KMS with shop-key. New receipts will be encrypted with it; the ones already stored keep their SSE-S3. Then fly the kites.",
      "highlight": "resource:receipts",
      "pulse": ["bucket-encryption:receipts"],
      "advanceWhen": {
        "kind": "run-finished",
        "predicate": {
          "kind": "bucket-encrypted-with-customer-key",
          "bucketId": "receipts"
        }
      }
    },
    {
      "id": "kms-use",
      "text": "The uploads fizzled against the halo. With your own key, S3 asks KMS to use it as the web servers' role, and the key's default policy leaves that to IAM. On app-role's card, add: allow kms:Use on shop-key. Then fly.",
      "highlight": "resource:app-role",
      "pulse": ["role-statements:app-role"],
      "advanceWhen": {
        "kind": "run-finished",
        "predicate": {
          "kind": "flows-delivered",
          "flows": {
            "intent": "legitimate"
          }
        }
      }
    },
    {
      "id": "database",
      "text": "Now the orders database: not encrypted, and RDS sets encryption only when a database is created, so that setting is greyed out. On its card, replace it with an encrypted copy. Then fly.",
      "highlight": "resource:db",
      "pulse": ["database-encryption:db"],
      "advanceWhen": {
        "kind": "run-finished",
        "predicate": {
          "kind": "database-encrypted",
          "databaseId": "db"
        }
      }
    },
    {
      "id": "wrap",
      "text": "The receipts are closed to the internet, and both stores are [encrypted at rest](card:concept-encryption-at-rest): access control decides who reads, encryption protects the disks. No third star yet? Give the database's copy shop-key too: one key, one set of permissions, one log of every use.",
      "pulse": ["database-encryption:db"],
      "advanceWhen": {
        "kind": "level-complete"
      }
    }
  ],
